carla-support: hey team, getting a 422 on POST /v1/rerank when sending mixed documents+candidates. Not sure which shape is expected, can someone help?

mike-ml: can you paste the exact body and the API response (no creds)?

carla-support: here's the body I used: ```json
{
  "query": { "text": "refund policy for yearly subscriptions" },
  "documents": [
    {
      "id": "doc-1",
      "passages": [ { "text": "refunds within 30 days" } ],
      "candidates": [ { "id": "cand-1", "text": "refund policy" } ]
    },
    {
      "id": "doc-2",
      "passages": [ { "text": "contact support for refunds" } ],
      "candidates": [ { "id": "cand-2", "text": "support contact" } ]
    }
  ],
  "top_k": 5
}
```
and the API returned: ```json
{
  "error": {
    "type": "invalid_request",
    "message": "422 Unprocessable Entity: ambiguous input schema (found both top-level and nested candidate lists).",
    "request_id": "req_abc123"
  }
}
```

sam-cs: mixing top-level documents with per-document candidates is supported in some older compat layers, but the current rerank schema expects either: a) top-level "candidates" array (flattened) with a top-level "query" (string or object), or b) "documents" where each document has "passages" (text fields). Having "candidates" objects nested inside a "documents" entry can trigger the validation path that errors. Also note that "query" can be either a string or an object with "text"; inconsistency can confuse the parser.

jon-oncall: quick repro tips: try sending a flattened payload: ```json
{
  "query": "refund policy for yearly subscriptions",
  "candidates": [
    {"id":"cand-1", "text":"refund policy", "doc_id":"doc-1"},
    {"id":"cand-2", "text":"support contact", "doc_id":"doc-2"}
  ],
  "top_k": 5
}
```
That avoids the ambiguous nesting and matches the model input we use for per-candidate scoring. If you need per-passage context, put passages under documents and omit nested "candidates".

carla-support: ok I tried flattening to top-level candidates but now I get scores all 0.0 in the response (no errors). Example response: ```json
{
  "results": [
    {"candidate_id":"cand-1", "score": 0.0},
    {"candidate_id":"cand-2", "score": 0.0}
  ],
  "request_id": "req_def456"
}
```
Any idea why scores would be zeroed?

mike-ml: zeros typically indicate a model-side scoring failure or clipping (NaN -> 0). Check: model version in the response headers, tokenization logs, and that the payload length didn't exceed token limits. Also confirm content-type header (application/json). If you send "charset" variants some parsers hit a different codepath.

eng-oncall-bot: Incident runbook: https://redwood.internal/runbooks/rerank-schema-422 — includes common payload fixes and a validation tool you can POST your body to for a quick pre-check.

carla-support: aha — I was sending Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8 from our webhook proxy. After forcing Content-Type: application/json the request validated and scores look correct. Repro steps: 1) original request via proxy with charset -> 422 or 0.0; 2) direct curl with application/json -> success.

sam-cs: thanks — I'll open a docs PR to explicitly call out allowed shapes and the Content-Type gotcha. Also adding an example of per-document candidates vs flattened candidates.

jon-oncall: if you want clearer validation errors we can add a follow-up to surface which field triggered the schema branch. Ping me and I can prioritize a tiny change.

carla-support: great, thanks everyone! :tada:
